    "title": "pandas.Timestamp.unit — pandas 3.1.0.dev0 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Timestamp.unit#\n\n- Timestamp.unit#\n- The abbreviation associated with self._creso. This property returns a string representing the time unit of the Timestamp’s resolution. It corresponds to the smallest time unit that can be represented by this Timestamp object. The possible values are: - ‘s’ (second) - ‘ms’ (millisecond) - ‘us’ (microsecond) - ‘ns’ (nanosecond) \n  - Returns:\n    - str\n    - A string abbreviation of the Timestamp’s resolution unit: - ‘s’ for second - ‘ms’ for millisecond - ‘us’ for microsecond - ‘ns’ for nanosecond\n See also \n  - `Timestamp.resolution`\n  - Return resolution of the Timestamp.\n  - `Timedelta`\n  - A duration expressing the difference between two dates or times.\n Examples >>> pd.Timestamp(\"2020-01-01 12:34:56\").unit 'us' >>> pd.Timestamp(\"2020-01-01 12:34:56.123\").unit 'us' >>> pd.Timestamp(\"2020-01-01 12:34:56.123456\").unit 'us' >>> pd.Timestamp(\"2020-01-01 12:34:56.123456789\").unit 'ns'",
    "markdown_length": 940,
